    <h1>Is Forensic Genetic Genealogy Legal? What the DOJ Rules Actually Say</h1>
    <p class="hero__lede">FGG is legal &mdash; but federal rules treat a genetic match as a starting point, not a verdict. Here&rsquo;s what the DOJ actually requires.</p>

    <div class="post__content">
      <p>If you follow cold-case news, you&rsquo;ve seen the phrase: a suspect was &ldquo;identified through genetic genealogy.&rdquo; It sounds like science closed the case on its own. It didn&rsquo;t &mdash; and the rules that govern how investigators are allowed to use this technique are narrower, and more careful, than the headlines suggest.</p>
      <p>Here&rsquo;s what the rules actually say, where they apply, and why the most important sentence in them is about protecting people who are <em>not</em> guilty.</p>

      <h2 id="the-short-answer">The Short Answer</h2>
      <p>Forensic genetic genealogy (FGG) is legal, but it is governed &mdash; at the federal level &mdash; by a written policy that treats a genetic match as a starting point, not a verdict. The clearest statement of that rule comes from the U.S. Department of Justice&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.justice.gov/olp/page/file/1204386/dl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2019 interim policy on the technique</a>, which says a genetic association is &ldquo;used by law enforcement only as an investigative lead,&rdquo; and that <strong>&ldquo;a suspect shall not be arrested based solely on a genetic association&rdquo;</strong> generated by a genealogy service.</p>
      <p>That single sentence is the heart of this brick. Read on for exactly what it does &mdash; and does not &mdash; mean.</p>

      <h2 id="what-it-is-and-isnt">What the DOJ Policy Is (and Three Things It Is Not)</h2>
      <p>In September 2019 the Department of Justice approved an <strong>Interim Policy</strong> titled <a href="https://www.justice.gov/olp/page/file/1204386/dl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>Forensic Genetic Genealogical DNA Analysis and Searching</em></a>, effective November 1, 2019. It set out, for the first time in writing, when and how investigators working under DOJ may use genetic genealogy.</p>
      <p>Three caveats matter before anyone treats it as &ldquo;the law of the land&rdquo;:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>It is interim, not a final rule.</strong> The document is labeled an interim policy. It was meant to guide practice, not to serve as a permanent, finalized regulation. Treat it as the best available written federal standard &mdash; not the last word.</li>
        <li><strong>It binds federal and participating investigations &mdash; it is not a nationwide statute.</strong> The policy applies to Department of Justice components and cases involving Department resources and interests. It is not a federal criminal statute that automatically governs every police department in the country. <strong>State and local rules on genetic genealogy vary, and many are not covered by this policy at all.</strong> So &ldquo;shall not be arrested based solely on a genetic association&rdquo; is a DOJ policy rule &mdash; not a universal American law you can assume applies to any given local case.</li>
        <li><strong>It names no specific database.</strong> The policy speaks generally of &ldquo;publicly-available open-data personal genomics databases&rdquo; and &ldquo;direct-to-consumer genetic genealogy services.&rdquo; It does <strong>not</strong> name GEDmatch, FamilyTreeDNA, or any other service. When you see a specific database named in a news story, that&rsquo;s the reporting &mdash; not the DOJ policy.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="what-it-requires">What the Policy Actually Requires</h2>
      <p>Within that scope, the policy is strict. A few key requirements, straight from <a href="https://www.justice.gov/olp/page/file/1204386/dl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the document</a>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>CODIS comes first.</strong> Before FGG may even be attempted, the crime-scene profile must have been uploaded to CODIS (the national DNA database) and those searches must have <em>failed</em> to produce a confirmed match. FGG is a fallback for cases traditional DNA databases can&rsquo;t crack. (See our explainer on <a href="/codis-vs-forensic-genetic-genealogy/">how CODIS differs from forensic genetic genealogy</a>.)</li>
        <li><strong>It&rsquo;s limited to serious cases.</strong> The policy applies to unsolved violent crime where the sample is believed to be from the perpetrator, or to suspected-homicide unidentified human remains. It is not a tool for routine or minor cases.</li>
        <li><strong>Investigators must identify themselves.</strong> Agencies must identify as law enforcement to a genealogy service, and may only use services that give explicit public notice that law enforcement may search them.</li>
        <li><strong>A genetic match is only a lead.</strong> The policy states plainly that the genetic association &ldquo;is used by law enforcement only as an investigative lead,&rdquo; and that &ldquo;traditional genealogy research and other investigative work is needed to determine the true nature of any genetic association.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><strong>Confirmation is required before an arrest.</strong> If a suspect is identified, standard STR DNA typing must be performed and compared directly against the crime-scene profile already in CODIS. The genealogy hit does not confirm identity &mdash; a separate, direct DNA comparison does. (This is the whole point of our brick on whether <a href="/is-fgg-evidence-or-just-a-lead/">FGG is evidence or an investigative lead</a>.)</li>
        <li><strong>Data gets handled and destroyed under rules.</strong> The policy sets out consent requirements for collecting reference samples from third parties, and rules for removing and destroying genetic profiles and account data depending on how a case resolves.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="the-role-of-consent">The Role of Consent: Opt-In Databases</h2>
      <p>The genealogy services investigators are permitted to use are built on <strong>consent</strong>. <a href="https://www.gedmatch.com/join-the-genetic-witness-program/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GEDmatch</a>, for example, runs an opt-in model: users choose whether to allow their uploaded genealogical data to be compared against law-enforcement profiles, and can change that setting. In its own words, the service lets users &ldquo;opt in and enable law enforcement to solve violent crimes and exonerate the falsely accused,&rdquo; and notes that law enforcement does not receive a user&rsquo;s raw DNA data when they opt in &mdash; only the same limited match information any user would see.</p>
      <p>This is the consent backbone that the DOJ policy&rsquo;s &ldquo;explicit notice&rdquo; requirement leans on: investigators are directed to use only services that tell their users, plainly, that law enforcement may search them.</p>

      <h2 id="the-privacy-debate">The Privacy Debate &mdash; Fairly, Both Sides</h2>
      <p>FGG is genuinely contested, and reasonable people land in different places. Laid out plainly:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The concern.</strong> When you upload your DNA to a genealogy site, you may be exposing genetic relatives &mdash; cousins, second cousins, people you&rsquo;ve never met &mdash; to a law-enforcement search they never consented to. Critics argue that an individual&rsquo;s opt-in can implicate an entire extended family, and that the technique&rsquo;s rules are still evolving and unevenly enforced across jurisdictions.</li>
        <li><strong>The counter-argument.</strong> Supporters note that FGG operates on consent-based, opt-in databases; that it is a last resort after CODIS fails; that it has both convicted the guilty and, as GEDmatch itself emphasizes, helped exonerate the falsely accused; and that the DOJ policy&rsquo;s confirmation-and-destruction rules are designed to limit misuse.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We&rsquo;re not here to settle that debate. The point of this hub is to make sure you understand what the technology does and doesn&rsquo;t do before you decide where you stand.</p>

      <h2 id="why-it-protects-the-innocent">Why the &ldquo;Only a Lead&rdquo; Rule Protects the Innocent</h2>
      <p>It&rsquo;s tempting to read &ldquo;shall not be arrested based solely on a genetic association&rdquo; as red tape. It isn&rsquo;t. It&rsquo;s a safeguard for people who are <em>not</em> guilty.</p>
      <p>A genealogy match points at a <em>family branch</em>, not a person. Everyone on that branch &mdash; including the completely innocent &mdash; can look, at first, like a candidate. The requirement that investigators do traditional genealogy, gather non-genetic evidence, and confirm with a direct DNA comparison before an arrest is what keeps an innocent relative from being charged because of who they&rsquo;re related to. The rule that a match is &ldquo;only an investigative lead&rdquo; is, in practice, a rule about not accusing the wrong person.</p>
      <p>That&rsquo;s the dignity thread that runs through this whole hub: the same care that gives a family answers is the care that protects the innocent from becoming a headline.</p>

      <p class="post__note"><strong>Sources:</strong> U.S. Department of Justice, <a href="https://www.justice.gov/olp/page/file/1204386/dl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Interim Policy: Forensic Genetic Genealogical DNA Analysis and Searching</a> (approved Sept. 2, 2019; effective Nov. 1, 2019); <a href="https://www.gedmatch.com/join-the-genetic-witness-program/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GEDmatch &mdash; the Genetic Witness Program</a> (opt-in consent framework). This is a plain-English explainer, not legal advice.</p>

